I\'m focusing on `TopNav`, `Settings`, and `Dashboard`. For `Sidebar`, I\'ll update text colors to `slate-700` and active backgrounds to `emerald-200`. The logo will be a shield with \"LivestockGuard\" and \"Enterprise Admin\" text. I\'ll implement brand colors using `emerald-800` for primary and `emerald-100` for active states in Tailwind. The `Login` view will be a split layout, with an image on the left and a form on the right. I\'m going to set up basic routing in `App.tsx` using state management to handle authentication and view selection, and also will build a simple settings page. I will execute concurrently, since it is time efficient.\n\n\n**Refactoring the Sidebar**\n\nI\'m now changing `Sidebar.tsx` to match \"LivestockGuard\" design principles, switching to light mode. I\'m swapping icon libraries from Material Symbols to Lucide icons that provide a similar look and feel, and then creating corresponding components, namely `Login`, `Settings`, and `DashboardOverview`. I\'m updating `Sidebar` and `App.tsx` files and addressing Tailwind classes for styling, while keeping the design in mind. I\'m focusing on concurrent development for better time management.\n\n\n**Integrating Views**\n\nI\'m now integrating the dashboard views. I will be rendering `UserTable` inside the `Users Management` view, which fetches data. For the `Dashboard` view, I\'ll display top stats. I\'m refining `Login.tsx`, `Settings.tsx`, `Sidebar.tsx`, and `App.tsx` files to manage authentication, views, and navigation. I\'m taking `currentView` and `setCurrentView` in `Sidebar.tsx`. I\'ll focus on concurrent execution.\n\n\n**Structuring the Components**\n\nI\'m now restructuring the layout to match the provided screenshots. I\'m focusing on the `UserTable` component, adjusting its styling to fit the \"Admin Dashboard\" layout, particularly the table design and background. I\'ll integrate the stats cards above the table, likely managing this within `App.tsx` when the `currentView` is \"dashboard\". I\'m also preparing the content for `Login.tsx` with input fields and icons.\n\n\n**Finalizing the Login**\n\nI\'ve just finalized the login logic and UI. I\'ve integrated authentication with a simulated API call, checking for identifier and password. The layout remains a split view, with an image on the left and a form on the right. I\'m focusing on user experience, ensuring a smooth transition to the dashboard upon successful login. The image will be displayed on the left and the login form will be on the right.\n\n\n**Refining Authentication Flow**\n\nI\'m now refining the authentication flow and the appearance of the user interface. I have been updating the styling of the login form to align with the provided design. I am focused on displaying an image on the left and form on the right. I\'m also addressing the overall user experience, including smooth transitions after login.\n\n\n**Implementing the Login Form**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the login form. I\'m implementing input fields for username/phone and password, along with error handling. I\'m styling these inputs using Tailwind CSS to match the design specifications, paying close attention to the visual cues and responsiveness. I am also working on adding icons for better user experience.\n\n\n**Finalizing the Components**\n\nI\'m now building out the components for Login and Dashboard. I\'m incorporating the final touches, like styling the input field and integrating the final visual elements. I\'m focusing on responsiveness and user-friendliness, to match the provided designs. I\'m also preparing for final testing and implementation.\n\n\n\n**Polishing the Login**\n\nI\'m now putting the finishing touches on the login form, focusing on the visual details. I\'m adding a \"Remember Me\" checkbox and a \"Forgot Password\" link with appropriate Tailwind classes. I\'m including proper visual cues and responsiveness. I\'ve also incorporated a submit button with a loading state, ensuring a seamless user experience. I\'m prioritizing user experience and ensuring everything looks sharp.\n\n\n**Enhancing Form Interactions**\n\nI\'m now integrating enhanced user interactions with the login form, including a submit button with a loading state, visually confirming the login attempt. I\'ve incorporated the \"Remember Me\" checkbox, and a \"Forgot Password\" link to improve usability and also added visual elements for the \"Contact Support\" link. I\'m focusing on these smaller visual enhancements and prioritizing user experience.\n\n\n**Refining the Settings**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the settings section, primarily the navigation for system configurations. I\'m implementing a sidebar with distinct options like profile details, notifications, and security preferences. I\'ll make the first option \"Profile Details\" as default. Each option should lead to a section for the corresponding component. I\'m using Lucide icons to add visual cues and improve the layout.\n\n\n**Structuring Settings Navigation**\n\nI\'m now building out the navigation within the settings view, using a sidebar design. I\'m prioritizing clarity and ease of access to profile details, security settings, and API keys. I am ensuring that the visual cues with Lucide icons improve the user experience.\n\n\n**Developing Profile Details**\n\nI am now focusing on the \'Profile Details\' section within the settings. I am structuring the information and building the UI to accommodate avatar changes and input fields for first name, last name, and email address. I\'m leveraging Tailwind CSS classes to manage the look and feel, and am now adding labels for enhanced user experience. I am currently working on styling the layout with grid columns for organization.\n\n\n**Implementing Profile Details**\n\nI\'m now building the \'Profile Details\' form within the settings view, focusing on the UI elements for updating user information. I\'m adding input fields for first name, last name, and email address, and an avatar. I am implementing the styles and the behavior. I will finalize labels, with Tailwind CSS classes, and also save changes for the user\'s data.\n\n```typescript\n{<LayoutDashboard size={20} />} , path: \'/dashboard\' },\n      { id: \'users\', label: \'Users Management\', icon: <Users size={20} />, path: \'/users\' },\n      { id: \'add-user\', label: \'Add User\', icon: <UserPlus size={20} />, path: \'/add-user\' },\n      { id: \'settings\', label: \'Settings\', icon: <Settings size={20} />, path: \'/settings\' },\n    ];\n  return (\n    <aside className=\"w-64 bg-white shadow-md h-screen\">\n      <div className=\"flex items-center justify-center h-16 bg-emerald-800 text-white font-semibold\">\n        <ShieldCheck size={24} className=\"mr-2\" />\n        LivestockGuard Enterprise Admin\n      </div>\n      <nav className=\"p-4\">\n        <ul>\n          {navItems.map((item) => (\n            <li key={item.id} className=\"mb-2\">\n              <button\n                onClick={() => onNavigate(item.id)}\n                className={`flex items-center w-full px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium rounded-md hover:bg-emerald-100 transition duration-200 ${\n                  currentView === item.id ? \'bg-emerald-200 text-emerald-800\' : \'text-slate-700\'\n                }`}\n              >\n                <span className=\"mr-2\">{item.icon}</span>\n                {item.label}\n              </button>\n            </li>\n          ))}\n          <li key=\"logout\" className=\"mt-4\">\n            <button\n              onClick={onLogout}\n              className=\"flex items-center w-full px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium rounded-md hover:bg-emerald-100 transition duration-200 text-slate-700\"\n            >\n              <span className=\"mr-2\"><LogOut size={20} /></span>\n              Logout\n            </button>\n          </li>\n        </ul>\n      </nav>\n    </aside>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n**Refining the Sidebar**\n\nI\'m now iterating on the `Sidebar` component. I\'ve updated the icons to Lucide, and implemented the \"LivestockGuard\" branding with a green theme. The sidebar highlights the active tab correctly, and includes the \"Reports\" option as inactive. I\'ve also swapped to a light-mode styling.\n\n\n**Updating the Sidebar**\n\nI\'m now refining the sidebar, updating its active states and overall styling. I\'m focusing on improved visual cues and user-friendly interactions, updating the design and implementing changes. I\'ve switched the text class, added `hover:text-emerald-900` class, and optimized the design.\n\n\n**Restructuring the Dashboard**\n\nI\'m now restructuring the dashboard layout and extracting components to refine the structure and improve organization. I\'m moving the top bar and stat cards from the dashboard logic directly into `App.tsx`, and will create `DashboardStats.tsx` for the cards. I\'m focusing on improved maintainability and clarity.\n\n\n**Refining the Admin Dashboard**\n\nI\'m now integrating the dashboard content within `App.tsx`, and building the `DashboardStats.tsx` component. I\'m dynamically adjusting the dashboard heading, based on the `currentUserRole` within the app, and displaying appropriate information and calls. I\'m focusing on user access and data, in the components.\n\n\n\n**Finalizing the Components**\n\nI\'m now building out the `DashboardStats` component, following the UI specifications to properly render the stats cards and dynamically adapt to user roles. I\'m focusing on user access and data management, and the `AdminDashboardHeading` component. I\'m now integrating the admin section, and will verify the content dynamically.\n\n\n**Finalizing the Components**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `DashboardStats` component, with the visual details for the provided designs. I\'ve built out the stat cards and ensured they render correctly. I\'m dynamically updating the `AdminDashboardHeading` and the user content. I will proceed with the components integration and their