  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Assessing the Feedback**\n\nI\'ve taken the user\'s positive feedback on the UI to heart. It\'s incredibly encouraging! Now, I\'m diving into the rule change request: a single super admin. My focus is on implementing this condition, ensuring only one such role can exist. I\'m strategizing how to best enforce this constraint within the system.\n\n\n**Implementing Super Admin Limit**\n\nI\'m now implementing the single super admin constraint. The dashboard will reflect this; the stat card will update. Additionally, I\'m addressing the \"Add New User\" button bug/feature request. The button needs functionality, connecting to a new user creation form. I\'m focusing on server-side changes now, modifying mock data to have only one Superadmin. I\'ll create a `POST /api/users` endpoint and integrate validation to prevent the creation of additional Superadmin roles.\n\n\n**Developing Add User Feature**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in developing the \"Add User\" feature. I\'ve started by creating the UI components, specifically `AddUser.tsx`, with fields for username, role, phone, email, and password. I\'m focusing on integrating this new component into the app, updating the sidebar navigation and app routing. The dashboard stat card is getting a dynamic update, reflecting the system\'s single Super Admin constraint.\n\n\n**Refining Dashboard Data**\n\nI\'m currently streamlining the hardcoded stats for visual clarity; I\'ll change the Super Admin count to \"1\" in the mock data. Also, removing the button to create a second Super Admin; it\'s not needed, and it\'s confusing.\n\n\n**Validating Super Admin Creation**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the server-side validation for new user creation, particularly regarding the Super Admin role. I\'ll craft a `POST /api/users` endpoint and incorporate validation to reject attempts to create a second Super Admin. On success, I plan to display a success message or redirect to the user list. For error handling, I\'ll visually highlight any backend errors. The role dropdown will initially include \"Superadmin\", so the user sees the validation error if they try.\n\n\n**Integrating User Table Updates**\n\nI\'m now integrating updates to the user table to display newly added users, which I\'ve confirmed is already handled within `useEffect`. I\'ll also address the \"Add New User\" button; it appears the table component doesn\'t have it anymore and shows the \"Admin Directory\" header, so there\'s no need to handle it. I\'m focusing on refining the `server.ts` changes to ensure the table displays the updated data correctly.\n\n\n**Implementing Server-Side Rules**\n\nI\'m now implementing the server-side logic for new user creation. I\'ve added a check to prevent non-Superadmins from creating Superadmins. I also added a check to ensure only one Superadmin exists. If a user is created, the new user will be added to the top of the user list.\n\n\n**Finalizing New User Form**\n\nI\'m now focusing on finalizing the new user form. I need to implement state management for all the input fields and establish that submitting the form triggers a fetch request to the `/api/users` endpoint. Furthermore, the UI needs to automatically navigate back to the user list upon successful user creation. I\'ll also modify the hardcoded \"8\" to \"1\" in `App.tsx` and adjust `currentUserRole` logic. Finally, I\'ll update all the files as specified to integrate the new functionality.\n\n\n**Refactoring App Navigation**\n\nI\'m now refactoring the app\'s navigation and data to reflect the changes. I\'ve removed the redundant Super Admin from the mock data, leaving only one in the mock user data. I\'ve also eliminated the \"Add Second Super Admin\" button, and I\'m updating the `Stats` component in `App.tsx` and the hardcoded count. Finally, I\'m streamlining the `renderContent` logic to properly navigate to the `AddUser` form when needed.\n\n\n**Implementing UI Form**\n\nI\'m now implementing the UI form, using standard `lucide-react` icons. I\'m focusing on the `AddUser.tsx` implementation, and connecting the \"Add User\" view, as well as updating the state card. Also, I need to execute `server.ts` changes to add the POST endpoint and fix the initial DB state.\n\n\n**Preparing Server Update**\n\nI\'m now preparing to update `server.ts` to reflect the single super admin constraint and other needed changes. I\'ll use `multi_edit_file` for this, targeting both the initial list of users and the position after the delete route. The current `users` list has two Superadmins, which I\'ll reduce to just one. My goal is to streamline the backend logic.\n\n\n**Constructing UI & Server Logic**\n\nI\'m now focused on constructing the UI form and its server-side integration. I\'ll craft `AddUser.tsx` with input fields and `lucide-react` icons. Then, I\'ll use `edit_file` to fix the button and stats in `App.tsx`. The goal is a functional new user form connected to the POST endpoint. I\'ll also use `create_file` to establish this feature.\n\n\n**Finalizing Form Submission**\n\nI\'m now implementing form submission and state management. I\'ve integrated a loading state with `setIsSubmitting`. I am handling form validation, comparing password and confirmation. On successful creation of a new user, I am fetching and updating the user data, navigating back to the user list, and updating the state and UI. Error messages are displayed for incorrect data or fetch failures.\n\n\n**Refining Submission Logic**\n\nI\'m now refining the submission logic, including disabling the submission while fetching to prevent double submissions. Also, I\'m integrating password confirmation with frontend validation. The successful creation of a new user is handled; the UI updates, data is refreshed, and I\'ve now integrated error message display.\n\n\n**Implementing Form States**\n\nI\'m now implementing the state management for each form field. I am handling all necessary input states for the new user form. These include username, role, password, and email. The next focus is implementing validation. I am working on how to integrate this frontend functionality with the server-side, and on integrating the form submission with the `/api/users` endpoint.\n\n\n**Validating Email and Phone**\n\nI\'m now focusing on validating the email and phone number input fields, adding this logic to the front end to improve form completeness. I will start by validating the email format using a basic regular expression. I am also working on adding this function to the form state. My goal is to ensure data integrity before submission and enhance the overall user experience.\n\n\n\n**Enhancing Password Visibility**\n\nI\'m now integrating a password visibility toggle within the \"Add User\" form. I\'m focusing on UI elements to let the user view their entered password. The button\'s functionality, its appearance, and its state management will be central. I\'m using state to control password visibility, and ensuring a smooth user experience.\n\n\n**Adding Password Visibility**\n\nI\'m now integrating a password visibility toggle, enhancing the user\'s experience by offering a clear view of their input. I\'m focusing on crafting the UI and ensuring a smooth toggle using state, making sure the user can readily see and manage their passwords. I am now working on making the password strength indicator more intuitive.\n\n\n**Validating Confirmation Passwords**\n\nI\'m now implementing confirmation password validation to the form, ensuring the new password field and the confirmation field match. I\'m focusing on providing visual feedback to the user on success and on error. I\'m updating my error state to reflect if password and confirmation match. I\'ll display the error message on the UI.\n\n\n\n**Integrating Save Button**\n\nI\'m now integrating the \"Save User\" button with a loading state, visually indicating submission progress. This will provide users with clear feedback during the saving process. I\'m also addressing the sidebar and the new user screen. The goal is to improve the user experience and ensure the system behaves as expected.\n\n\n\n**Orchestrating UI & Server**\n\nI\'m now orchestrating the UI and server logic for the \"Add User\" feature. I\'ve broken it down into orderly steps: first, a new file with the user form; second, enabling navigation and text cleanup, followed by editing `App.tsx` and adding the new route; finally, a backend `server.ts` update to include API routes and server-side rules. I\'m focusing on exact code blocks to make this precise.\n\n\n\n**Refining Superadmin Handling**\n\nI\'m now refining the mock user data in the backend server to adhere to the single Superadmin constraint. The existing user list contained two Superadmins, which I\'ve reduced to one to ensure the system\'s rule is followed. This change is a simple but important update for data consistency and integrity.\n\n\n\n**Securing Delete Endpoint**\n\nI\'m now focusing on securing the delete endpoint with a key server-side role validation check. I\'ve integrated a check for the user\'s role before allowing deletion. The user will be forbidden from deleting the user if they\'re not a Superadmin. The server will send an appropriate status message.\n\n\n**Securing Create User Logic**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the create user logic. I\'ve integrated a check to ensure that only a Superadmin can assign the Superadmin role and a constraint to ensure only one superadmin can exist. The server-side code now rejects the creation of additional Superadmins if one already exists. I have also added an `authenticateRole` middleware.\n\n\n\n**Refining Backend User Creation**\n\nI\'m now implementing the user creation with the server. I\'m focusing on user ID generation. I\'ve updated `server.ts` to add a new `POST` route for users, and a new `Sidebar.tsx`. I\'ve added a check to the user\'s role before allowing deletion. I\'ve added an `authenticateRole` middleware. I\'m setting the