  file_change {
    absolute_path_uri: "file:///README.md"
    file_change_type: FILE_CHANGE_TYPE_CREATE
    replacement_chunks {
      replacement_content: "# LivestockGuard Enterprise Admin Portal\n\n**GitHub Description:** A secure, role-based enterprise administration dashboard for livestock monitoring and IoT IoT field operator management.\n\n## Features Summary\n\n- **Role-Based Access Control (RBAC):** Distinct privileges for standard Operators, basic Admins, and a strictly enforced single Super Admin.\n- **Authentication Interface:** Secure login simulation tailored for \"Earth-First Security\" agricultural operations.\n- **User Management Operations:** Full CRUD interface for managing active field personnel and enterprise administrators.\n- **System Settings:** Centralized hub for profile administration, notification preferences, security credential updates, and API Key lifecycle management.\n- **Security & Activity Reporting:** Real-time online administrator tracking, critical security alerting, and comprehensive user activity audit logs.\n\n---\n\n## \360\237\232\200 Team Developer Guide\n\nEverything you need to take this local prototype to a production-ready application.\n\n### 1. Opening in VS Code\n1. Unzip the downloaded project folder.\n2. Open your terminal or command prompt.\n3. Navigate into the unzipped folder: `cd path/to/unzipped-folder`\n4. Type `code .` to open the directory directly in VS Code.\n5. Open the integrated terminal in VS Code (`Ctrl` + `~`).\n6. Run `npm install` to ensure all dependencies are ready.\n7. Run `npm run dev` to start the local development server.\n\n### 2. Version Control (Pushing to GitHub)\nTo version control this project, run the following commands sequentially in your VS Code terminal:\n\n```bash\ngit init\ngit add .\ngit commit -m \"Initial commit: LivestockGuard Admin Dashboard\"\ngit branch -M main\n```\n\nNext, go to GitHub, create a new empty repository, copy its HTTPS/SSH URL, and run:\n```bash\ngit remote add origin <YOUR_GITHUB_REPO_URL>\ngit push -u origin main\n```\n\n### 3. Connecting a Real Database\n\nRight now, the application uses an Express server (`server.ts`) with an in-memory mock JSON array (`let users = [...]`) to simulate database transactions. Your backend engineers can easily swap this out for a real database (PostgreSQL, MySQL, etc.).\n\n**Implementation Steps:**\n1. **Choose an ORM (e.g., Prisma):**\n   ```bash\n   npm install prisma --save-dev\n   npm install @prisma/client\n   npx prisma init\n   ```\n2. **Configure your Database URL:** Update the `.env` file generated by Prisma with your actual database connection string.\n3. **Migrate `server.ts`:** Locate the Express API endpoints in `server.ts` (like `app.get(\'/api/users\')` or `app.post(\'/api/users\')`). Replace the mock array manipulation with real database queries (e.g., `await prisma.user.findMany()`).\n4. **Implement Real Security:** Replace the `x-mock-role` header logic in the `authenticateRole` middleware with a real JWT (JSON Web Token) or session validation library.\n\n### 4. What are the API Keys for?\nIn the settings panel, you\'ll see an \"API Keys\" section. \nAPI keys are secure, non-human passwords. They allow external systems\342\200\224like **IoT smart collars on your livestock, automated weather stations, drones, or third-party farming software**\342\200\224to send automated data to your database without needing to manually log in through the application interface."
    }
